 "note": [
  "The English teen edition quotes the KJV; every chapter epigraph is Louis Segond (1910) verbatim (whole verse, or the matching portion for Hebrews 10:19 and 12:2a), the “(KJV)” label is dropped, and the attribution's KJV sentence is replaced with the Segond sentence used by the other French teen editions. English Psalm 63:8, 34:8, 57:5 and 46:10 are Segond 63:9, 34:9, 57:6 and 46:11; the epigraphs cite the Segond numbers while the reference rows keep English numbering.",
  "Segond renders “meek” as « débonnaires » in Matthew 5:5 but « doux » in Matthew 11:29, so ch. 10 bridges the two words explicitly; “Be thou exalted” is Segond's « Élève-toi » (ch. 9 title), so “three words from the Psalms” became « une courte prière tirée des Psaumes ». John 3:14-15 is quoted as Segond has it (its 3:15 lacks the KJV's “should not perish”).",
  "Tozer's prose and prayers are rendered fresh; God is addressed with « tu », divine pronouns are lower case, and Tozer's capitalised nouns Presence/Voice stay « Présence », « Voix ». “I want to want Thee” is « Je désire te désirer » to keep the doubling (ch. 2 title, description, Q&A). Tozer speaking to his adult readers keeps « vous »; the edition's own voice uses « tu » like the other teen editions.",
  "Names: « Alliance chrétienne et missionnaire », « l’église Southside Alliance » (kept), « Le Nuage d’inconnaissance », Augustine and the Westminster catechism in their customary French wording. Ch. 2 says God spoke Numbers 18:20 to “the tribe of Levi”; the verse is addressed to Aaron (Levi's priestly line), kept as written. The title « À la poursuite de Dieu (Pour ados) » is 34 characters, above the 28-character guideline, and may need a shorter rail label.",
  "AI-translated in-session; awaiting native review."
 ],
